<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<!-- Apple requires this file for App Store submission, and it is the app's own
     public statement about what it does with data. Every entry below is traced
     to a specific call site, and `IOSPrivacyManifestTests` re-derives the list
     from the source so an added API or a removed one cannot leave this file
     quietly wrong.

     Over-declaring is as much a false statement as under-declaring, so nothing
     here is included "to be safe". Active keyboard, and the file timestamp APIs
     beyond the one below, are absent because this app does not call them.

     Note the Share extension has its OWN manifest, with a shorter list: it
     never reaches the network and never touches user defaults. -->
<dict>
	<!-- Nothing here tracks. There is no analytics SDK, no advertising
	     identifier and no third-party network destination in this binary, so
	     there are no tracking domains to declare either.

	     One entry below carries Apple's ANALYTICS purpose, and that is not a
	     contradiction: Apple's "tracking" means linking this app's data to
	     third-party data for advertising or measurement brokering, while
	     "Analytics" is what a purpose-built, first-party, identifier-free
	     aggregate is FOR. The aggregate in question is written by Relayium's own
	     server, carries no identifier of any kind, and reaches nobody else. -->
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array>
		<!-- A name the user types, or hands over once through Apple. Two call
		     sites on this platform, and both are the person's own words:

		       · the create-account form's own field — `SignInView`'s
		         `TextField(L10n.t(.loginDisplayName), text: $draft.displayName)`,
		         sent by `AccountClient.register` as `"displayName": displayName`
		         and kept in `users.display_name`;
		       · Sign in with Apple — `credential.fullName` on the FIRST
		         authorization only, formatted by `AppleSignInName.format` and
		         posted to `api/auth/apple/native` as `"name": name`. Apple sends
		         it once and empty afterwards, and empty is what the server gets:
		         a name derived from the address would be one this app invented.

		     **The device label is deliberately not part of this entry.** On macOS
		     the app sends `Host.current().localizedName` — the computer name,
		     which macOS usually seeds from the owner's full name, and which is
		     therefore a name. iOS cannot and does not do that:
		     `AppEnvironment.deviceName()` resolves to `deviceFamilyName(
		     forModelIdentifier:)`, which answers "iPhone", "iPad" or "iPod touch"
		     and nothing else. A hardware family is not a name, and declaring it as
		     one here would describe a personal detail this build never sends. -->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeName</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<!-- The email address a user types to register or sign in, sent to
		     Relayium's own server (`POST /api/auth/register`,
		     `/api/auth/email/resend` — `AccountClient.swift`). Linked to the
		     account by definition, since it IS the account identifier.

		     **The files themselves are deliberately not listed, and that is not
		     an omission.** They are encrypted on the device and the server sees
		     ciphertext, so no file content or file metadata is collected in
		     Apple's sense. That is the product's central promise, and stating it
		     here is the same claim the website makes. -->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeEmailAddress</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<!-- StoreKit's signed transaction is submitted to Relayium and retained as
		     the account's subscription source, plan, status and renewal period. -->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurchaseHistory</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<!-- Relayium's per-account appAccountToken is sent to Apple with a
		     purchase and retained in both parties' transaction/account records. -->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeUserID</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<!-- Metering: how much of the service this account used, which is activity
		     in the app and therefore Apple's "Other Usage Data". Four call sites
		     produce it on this platform, and each is retained past the request it
		     served:

		       · uploads — `HTTPResumableTransport` (`api/uploads`, the chunk PATCH
		         and `/finalize`) makes the server meter the bytes (`files.go`,
		         `RecordMeter(… MeterUpload …)` → `usage_monthly`) and keep the
		         ciphertext's `stored_files.size` against the storage quota;
		       · stored downloads — `CloudClient` (`api/files/<id>/blob`) meter the
		         same way (`RecordMeter(… MeterDownload …)`);
		       · relayed bytes — `HTTPICEClient` (`api/ice`) takes TURN credentials
		         whose username carries `<owner>.<code>` (`turn.go`), so a node's
		         heartbeat books that allocation's bytes to the account (`nodes.go`,
		         `RecordUsage` → `usage_events`/`usage_periods`);
		       · **the Device Inbox delivery this release adds** — `InboxClient.blob`
		         fetches `api/devices/<id>/inbox/tasks/<task>/blob`, and
		         `deviceinbox_task.go` meters the bytes it served with
		         `RecordMeter(… MeterDownload …)` into the same `usage_monthly`.
		         Receiving a file on iPhone or iPad therefore adds to the account's
		         monthly total, and saying so is the point of this bullet.

		     Byte counts and timestamps only — never what was transferred, which the
		     server holds as ciphertext. They are kept as per-month, per-account
		     totals because that is what enforces the quotas included in the plan,
		     and the app reads them back through `AccountClient.fetchUsage`
		     (`api/me/usage`). That account-linked metering is Other Usage Data for
		     App Functionality; the identifier-free activation aggregate below is a
		     different thing and is declared separately. -->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeOtherUsageData</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<!-- First-party, identifier-free activation aggregates. Showing a
		     cross-network code from this app mints one (`PairClient`, `api/pair`),
		     and the server counts three pairing actions by UTC month: a successful
		     code mint, the first admitted socket for that live code, and the first
		     transition to two admitted peers. It keeps only month, one closed stage
		     and a nonnegative count; `activation_funnel_monthly` has no event row,
		     exact timestamp, account, user, IP, code, room, device, session, token,
		     platform, locale or content field. Writes are bounded best-effort, so
		     the result is a monthly lower bound used to evaluate whether this
		     product flow works, not a user or cohort history. Apple classifies
		     retained information about interaction with a feature as Product
		     Interaction, and evaluation of feature effectiveness as Analytics, even
		     when identifiers are absent — so this entry is unlinked, and Analytics
		     rather than App Functionality. -->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeProductInteraction</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAnalytics</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<!-- **`NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeDeviceID` is deliberately absent, and the
		     macOS manifest declares it.** That difference is real, not an
		     oversight, and it is the entry most likely to be added "for parity":

		       · macOS sends `InstallationIdentity`'s 32 random bytes as
		         `install_id` when a BROWSER sign-in begins
		         (`HTTPDeviceAuthClient.start`, reached from
		         `AppEnvironment.makeBrowserLoginModel`). This app has no browser
		         sign-in path and never calls that client;
		       · the Apple purchase-continuation flow can post an `appInstanceId`
		         to `purchase-dispatch`/`purchase-outcome`. **This app does call
		         `purchase-dispatch`**, so "iOS never reaches it" would be the
		         wrong reason and would be false. The identifier is absent for a
		         narrower one: that field comes from a purchase CONTINUATION, only
		         the `.durableContinuationRequired` policy creates one,
		         `AppStoreDistribution` selects that policy on macOS and
		         `IOSAppleSubscriptions.makeModel` names no policy at all — so it
		         takes the default `.legacyOneShot`, `continuation` is nil, and
		         `AccountClient.dispatchApplePurchase` OMITS the field rather than
		         sending it empty.

		     Linking a module is not calling it, and calling an endpoint is not
		     sending every field it accepts — so `IOSPrivacyManifestTests` asserts
		     this from the iOS source, the policy default and the encoder, rather
		     than from the module list. If iOS ever adopts purchase continuation,
		     this entry becomes required. -->
	</array>
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array>
		<!-- `VerificationPreference` stores whether this device blocks on the
		     SAS comparison, in `UserDefaults.standard` — this app's own domain,
		     readable by nothing else. `SharedDraftInbox` records which shared
		     drafts have been handed to the send flow, in the same place. -->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>CA92.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<!-- `SharedDraftStore.stale(_:grace:now:)` reads `.modificationDate` to
		     decide whether an interrupted draft is old enough to sweep. The files
		     are inside the app group container and nowhere else, which is exactly
		     what DDA9.1 covers. -->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryFileTimestamp</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>DDA9.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<!-- `WebRTCLinkTransport` measures its establishment deadlines against
		     `ProcessInfo.systemUptime` rather than a wall clock, so that changing
		     the device's date cannot expire a live connection. That is elapsed
		     time between events inside the app, which is 35F9.1. -->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategorySystemBootTime</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>35F9.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<!-- `InboxSpace.freeBytes` calls `statfs` on the receive folder before a
		     Device Inbox delivery is written, so a transfer that cannot fit is
		     refused rather than allowed to fill the disk on its way to failing
		     (`InboxFailure.swift`). That is exactly E174.1: checking whether
		     there is sufficient disk space to write files.

		     E174.1 attaches a condition, and this call site meets it — the free
		     byte count is compared against the delivery's size, decides a local
		     refusal, and is not sent off device, stored, or derived from in any
		     form that leaves this app's container. -->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryDiskSpace</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>E174.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
